<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.2.4: Solving a Real-World Problem Using Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02111</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.2.4: Solving a Real-World Problem Using Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>86e36e35-bc35-42ee-b546-462bd5e17e28</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A movie theater models the revenue from ticket sales in one day as a function of the ticket price, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Here are two expressions defining the same revenue function.</para>
      <para id="para-00002"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00003"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">According to this model, how high would the ticket price have to be for the theater to make $0 in revenue? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Enter the ticket price and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">$30. If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then either <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. If the latter is a true equation, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> must be 30.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">What equation can you write to find out what ticket price(s) would allow the theater to make $600 in revenue?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Enter an equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
